 questions:mix([
  q(PROF,'Which step belongs to the pre-analytical phase of laboratory testing?','Collecting and labeling the specimen',['Running the sample on the analyzer','Reporting the result to the provider','Reviewing quality control after the run, before the next batch of patient samples'],'A','Most lab errors happen in the pre-analytical phase, such as wrong patient, wrong tube or poor handling.',EST),
  q(PROF,'What is a delta check?','A comparison of a patient’s result with their own previous result',['A check that the lab’s temperature is correct','A test run on a control sample','A check of the analyzer’s electrical supply'],'B','A sudden large change can signal a mislabeled specimen or a real change that needs attention.',EST),
  q(PROF,'How is a reference interval (normal range) usually defined?','The range that includes the middle 95% of results from healthy people',['The highest and lowest results ever seen in the lab','The range set by the patient’s doctor','The values that all patients must reach to be discharged'],'C','Because 5% of healthy people fall outside the range, a slightly abnormal result is not always disease.',EST),
  q(PROF,'A method gives very similar results each time the same sample is tested, but all are far from the true value. How is it described?','Precise but not accurate',['Accurate but not precise','Both accurate and precise','Neither, because repeated results always agree'],'D','Precision is about agreement between repeated results; accuracy is about closeness to the true value.',EST),
  q(SAFE,'Which biosafety level is needed for work with Ebola virus?','BSL-4',['BSL-1','BSL-2, the level used in most hospital labs','BSL-3'],'A','BSL-4 labs use full-body positive-pressure suits and isolated buildings for deadly agents with no cure.',ONE),
  q(SAFE,'What does the blue section of an NFPA diamond indicate?','Health hazard',['Flammability','Reactivity or instability','Special hazards such as water reactivity'],'B','Each section is rated 0 to 4, with 4 being the most severe hazard.',ONE),
  q(SAFE,'Which class of fire extinguisher is used for burning flammable liquids such as alcohol?','Class B',['Class A, for paper and wood','Class D','Class K'],'C','Class B covers flammable liquids and gases; many labs use ABC extinguishers that cover several types.',ONE),
  q(SAFE,'What are the usual autoclave conditions for sterilizing biohazardous waste?','About 121 °C at 15 psi for at least 15 to 30 minutes',['About 37 °C overnight in a standard incubator with the door kept closed','About 60 °C for 5 minutes','Room temperature for 1 hour'],'D','Steam under pressure kills bacteria, viruses and spores; indicator tape or spore tests confirm it worked.',ONE),
  q(SAFE,'Under the OSHA Bloodborne Pathogens Standard, how often must the Exposure Control Plan be reviewed?','At least once a year',['Every 10 years','Only when a new employee is hired','Never, once written'],'A','The plan describes how workers are protected and must be updated as new safer devices become available.',ONE),
  q(SAFE,'A lab worker has a latex allergy. Which type of gloves is a common safe choice?','Nitrile gloves',['Powdered latex gloves','Cotton gloves','No gloves at all'],'B','Nitrile gloves resist punctures and many chemicals and do not contain latex proteins.',ONE),
  q(MATH,'How many grams of NaCl (molecular weight 58.5) are needed to make 500 mL of a 0.5 M solution?','About 14.6 g',['About 29.3 g','About 58.5 g','About 117 g'],'C','Moles = 0.5 mol/L × 0.5 L = 0.25 mol, and 0.25 × 58.5 g = 14.6 g.',EST),
  q(MATH,'On a hemocytometer, 100 cells are counted in 4 large squares (each 1 mm² and 0.1 mm deep) with a 1:20 dilution. What is the count?','5,000 cells/µL',['500 cells/µL','2,000 cells/µL','50,000 cells/µL'],'D','Cells = count × dilution ÷ (area × depth) = 100 × 20 ÷ (4 × 0.1) = 5,000 per µL.',EST),
  q(MATH,'How many grams of solute are needed to make 250 mL of a 4% w/v solution?','10 g',['4 g','25 g','100 g'],'A','4% w/v means 4 g per 100 mL, so 250 mL × 0.04 = 10 g of solute brought to final volume.',EST),
  q(HEME,'Many small, round red cells without central pallor are seen on a smear. Which condition is most likely?','Hereditary spherocytosis',['Iron deficiency anemia','Sickle cell anemia','Lead poisoning'],'A','Spherocytes have lost membrane, making them less flexible so they are removed early by the spleen.',EST),
  q(HEME,'Target cells (codocytes) are most often seen with which conditions?','Thalassemia and liver disease',['Bacterial infection','Allergic reactions','Acute leukemia only'],'B','Target cells have extra membrane compared with their hemoglobin, giving a bull’s-eye look.',EST),
  q(HEME,'Howell-Jolly bodies are commonly found in patients who have had which procedure?','Removal of the spleen (splenectomy)',['Removal of the gallbladder','A tonsillectomy','A knee replacement'],'C','These small DNA fragments in red cells are normally removed by the spleen.',EST),
  q(HEME,'Auer rods seen in blast cells are most typical of which condition?','Acute myeloid leukemia',['Chronic lymphocytic leukemia','Infectious mononucleosis','Iron deficiency anemia'],'D','Auer rods are needle-shaped clumps of granules found in myeloid blasts.',EST),
  q(HEME,'Which white blood cell contains granules of histamine and heparin?','Basophil',['Neutrophil','Lymphocyte','Monocyte, the largest white cell'],'A','Basophils are the least common white cell and play a role in allergic responses.',EST),
  q(HEME,'Fibrinogen is also known as which clotting factor?','Factor I',['Factor II','Factor VIII','Factor X'],'B','Thrombin converts fibrinogen into fibrin strands that form the clot.',EST),
  q(HEME,'An older adult has a high lymphocyte count with many smudge cells on the smear. What is most likely?','Chronic lymphocytic leukemia',['Acute bacterial infection','Iron deficiency anemia','Polycythemia vera, which raises red cells'],'C','Smudge cells are fragile lymphocytes that break when the smear is made.',EST),
  q(IMM,'Which cells kill virus-infected and tumor cells without needing prior exposure?','Natural killer (NK) cells',['Plasma cells','Red blood cells','Platelets'],'D','NK cells are part of the innate immune system and act quickly without antibodies.',EST),
  q(IMM,'What is the main job of the complement system?','To help destroy pathogens by marking them and punching holes in their membranes',['To carry oxygen in the blood','To form blood clots that seal off damaged vessels after an injury to the skin','To make antibodies'],'A','Complement proteins also attract white cells and boost inflammation.',EST),
  q(IMM,'What is a monoclonal antibody?','An antibody made by a single clone of cells that targets one specific epitope',['A mixture of antibodies from many different cells','An antibody that targets every antigen','A type of white blood cell'],'B','Monoclonal antibodies are used in lab tests and in treatments for cancer and autoimmune disease.',EST),
  q(IMM,'A patient has type A blood. Which red cells can they safely receive?','Type A or type O',['Type B or type AB','Only type AB','Any type'],'C','Type A people have anti-B antibodies, so they cannot receive red cells with the B antigen.',EST),
  q(IMM,'A breastfed infant receives antibodies from the mother’s milk. Which type of immunity is this?','Passive natural immunity',['Active natural immunity','Active artificial immunity from a vaccine','Passive artificial immunity'],'D','Passive immunity is borrowed and short-lived because the infant did not make the antibodies.',EST),
  q(IMM,'What is the prozone effect in serology?','A false-negative result caused by too much antibody',['A false-positive result caused by too little antigen','A result that is always correct','A color change that happens too fast'],'B','Diluting the sample and retesting can reveal a positive result hidden by antibody excess.',EST),
  q(IMM,'Rapid strep and home COVID-19 tests use which test format?','A lateral flow immunoassay',['A Gram stain','Blood culture','A Western blot done in a reference lab over several days'],'C','Lateral flow tests move the sample along a strip where labeled antibodies form a visible line.',EST),
  q(IMM,'What does a positive purified protein derivative (PPD) skin test show?','Past or present exposure to tuberculosis',['An active strep infection','A food allergy','A blood type'],'D','A positive test does not prove active disease; a chest X-ray and other tests follow.',EST),
  q(UA,'Waxy casts in the urine sediment most often suggest which condition?','Advanced chronic kidney disease',['Normal exercise','A bladder infection only','Dehydration after a long run'],'A','Waxy casts form when urine flow is very slow through damaged tubules.',EST),
  q(UA,'White blood cell casts in the urine most strongly suggest which condition?','Pyelonephritis, an infection of the kidney',['A simple bladder infection with no kidney involvement','Kidney stones only','Normal urine'],'B','Casts form in the kidney tubules, so WBC casts show the inflammation is in the kidney itself.',EST),
  q(UA,'Which crystal looks like a “coffin lid” and forms in alkaline urine, often with infection?','Triple phosphate (struvite)',['Calcium oxalate','Uric acid','Cystine'],'C','Struvite stones are linked to urease-producing bacteria such as Proteus.',EST),
  q(UA,'Urobilinogen in urine is formed from which substance?','Bilirubin broken down by intestinal bacteria',['Glucose from the liver','Protein from the kidneys','Hemoglobin in the bladder'],'D','High urobilinogen can occur with liver disease or rapid red cell breakdown.',EST),
  q(CHEM,'Low serum albumin is most often linked to which conditions?','Liver disease or malnutrition',['Dehydration','Gout','Recent hard exercise, such as running a marathon the day before'],'A','Albumin is made by the liver and keeps fluid inside blood vessels.',EST),
  q(CHEM,'High cortisol levels are linked to which disorder?','Cushing syndrome',['Addison disease, which causes low cortisol','Hypothyroidism','Diabetes insipidus'],'B','Cortisol testing is often timed because levels normally change throughout the day.',EST),
  q(CHEM,'Which specimens are needed for a creatinine clearance test?','A timed 24-hour urine and a blood creatinine',['A single throat swab','A random spot urine only, collected at any time during the clinic visit','A stool sample'],'C','Creatinine clearance estimates how well the kidneys are filtering the blood.',EST),
  q(CHEM,'In therapeutic drug monitoring, when is a trough level drawn?','Right before the next dose',['Exactly at the time of the dose','One week after the last dose','Only after the patient eats'],'D','The trough is the lowest level, which shows whether the drug stays in the effective range.',EST),
  q(CHEM,'What is the normal pH range of arterial blood?','7.35–7.45',['6.8–7.0','7.6–7.8','5.0–6.0'],'A','A pH below 7.35 is acidosis, and above 7.45 is alkalosis; both can be life-threatening if severe.',EST),
  q(CHEM,'Which method is used on most analyzers to measure sodium and potassium?','Ion-selective electrodes',['Gram staining','Manual counting under a microscope','Agglutination'],'B','Each electrode responds to one ion and produces a voltage related to its concentration.',EST),
  q(CHEM,'Which is the main anion (negatively charged ion) outside the cells?','Chloride',['Potassium','Sodium, which is the main positively charged ion','Calcium'],'C','Chloride follows sodium and helps keep fluid and acid–base balance.',EST),
  q(MICRO,'Haemophilus influenzae needs which growth factors?','X factor (hemin) and V factor (NAD)',['Vitamin C and iron','Sugar and salt only','Oxygen and carbon dioxide only'],'D','These factors are released from lysed red cells in chocolate agar.',EST),
  q(MICRO,'Which organism grows in 6.5% salt broth and on bile esculin agar?','Enterococcus',['Streptococcus pyogenes','Neisseria gonorrhoeae','Haemophilus influenzae'],'A','Enterococci are hardy gut bacteria that often resist several antibiotics.',EST),
  q(MICRO,'Which organism gives a positive CAMP test and is screened for in pregnant women?','Streptococcus agalactiae (group B strep)',['Streptococcus pyogenes','Escherichia coli, which is a gram-negative rod','Staphylococcus epidermidis'],'B','Group B strep can cause serious infections in newborns, so mothers are screened near term.',EST),
  q(MICRO,'Which gram-negative rod is known for “swarming” across agar plates?','Proteus',['Klebsiella','Pseudomonas','Salmonella'],'C','Proteus is very motile and often causes urinary tract infections and stones.',EST),
  q(MICRO,'Which urease-positive organism is linked to stomach ulcers?','Helicobacter pylori',['Clostridium perfringens','Candida albicans','Streptococcus pneumoniae'],'D','H. pylori can be detected with breath, stool antigen or tissue tests.',EST),
  q(MICRO,'A mold shows septate hyphae that branch at sharp angles. Which genus is most likely?','Aspergillus',['Candida, which is a yeast','Cryptococcus','Saccharomyces'],'A','Aspergillus can cause lung infections in patients with weak immune systems.',EST),
  q(MICRO,'What does polymerase chain reaction (PCR) do?','Makes many copies of a specific DNA sequence so it can be detected',['Stains bacteria purple or pink','Measures antibiotic zone sizes on an agar plate after the bacteria grow overnight','Grows bacteria on agar for 48 hours'],'B','PCR can find organisms that are slow or hard to grow in culture.',EST),
  q(PARA,'A stool sample shows amoebae that have ingested red blood cells. Which parasite is this?','Entamoeba histolytica',['Giardia','Trichomonas vaginalis','Enterobius vermicularis'],'C','Ingested red cells separate this disease-causing amoeba from harmless ones.',EST),
  q(PARA,'Which insect transmits leishmaniasis?','The sand fly',['The kissing bug','The tsetse fly','The Anopheles mosquito'],'D','Leishmaniasis can cause skin sores or a serious disease of internal organs.',EST),
  q(PARA,'Chagas disease, caused by Trypanosoma cruzi, is spread by which insect?','The kissing (reduviid) bug',['The sand fly','The Aedes mosquito','The deer tick'],'A','The bug’s feces enter a bite or the eye, and the disease can damage the heart years later.',EST),
  q(PARA,'Which roundworm can reinfect the same person (autoinfection) and may cause severe disease in people taking steroids?','Strongyloides stercoralis',['Ascaris lumbricoides, the largest intestinal roundworm','Taenia solium','Giardia'],'B','Rhabditiform larvae, rather than eggs, are usually found in the stool of infected patients.',EST)
 ],9517)
